meta {
  name: Update User Permissions
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{invitedUserId}}/permissions
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "overrides": [
      { "moduleKey": "clients", "level": "read" }
    ]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("stores only the difference from role defaults", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("effectivePermissions");
    expect(body).to.have.property("roleDefaultPermissions");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /users/:userId/permissions — per-user overrides

  **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`).
  **Permission:** `agency:users:permissions`

  Runs against the invited row, for the same reason as *Change User Roles*.

  The caller submits the level they want per page; the service diffs each page
  against the user's **role defaults** and stores only the differences, as grant
  and revoke rows in `userPermissions`.

  That diff is the important part: a page left at its role default stores
  nothing, so the user keeps inheriting later changes to the role. Storing an
  absolute set would freeze them at today's permissions forever.

  ## Body

  | Field | Type | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | overrides | `{ moduleKey, level }[]` | `level` is `none` \| `read` \| `write`. Pages not listed keep their role default. Modules the agency has not enabled are ignored. |

  ## Only page permissions

  `agency:*` and `platform:*` can never be granted per user — they come from role
  membership alone. The catalog marks this as `assignableToUser: false`, and the
  service rejects anything else.

  ## Response

  `UserDetailResponse`, including `effectivePermissions` (what the user actually
  has) and `roleDefaultPermissions` (what their roles alone would give). The UI
  marks a page as overridden by comparing the two.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — a permission outside the page model, or the same
    permission both granted and revoked.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `agency:users:permissions`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such user in this agency.
}
